<TopicPage slug="subjects/math/advanced/logic-and-proof">
  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is an advanced Mathematics lesson — the craft manual behind every other lesson you
    have taken. It assumes the foundational <em>Euclidean Geometry</em> lesson, whose circle
    theorems this page dissects end to end, and the intermediate <em>Circles &amp; Coordinate
    Geometry</em> lesson where those theorems were first stated and used; the intermediate
    <em>Sequences &amp; Series</em> lesson supplies the sum formula the induction widget proves.
    No calculus is needed, though results you may know from <em>The Derivative</em> appear as
    examples of claims that came with receipts.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>use the logical vocabulary — propositions, connectives, implication, quantifiers — precisely enough to negate any statement correctly;</li>
    <li>keep converse, inverse and contrapositive straight, and name the two classic invalid inferences;</li>
    <li>run the four proof patterns — direct, contrapositive, contradiction, induction — and choose sensibly among them;</li>
    <li>deploy a counterexample to demolish a false universal claim, and explain why testing can never prove one;</li>
    <li>write clean proofs: claim and road map first, definitions load-bearing, every line justified, the diagram proposing but never disposing.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Every result you have met so far — Pythagoras, the derivative rules, the fundamental
    theorem — came with a receipt: a <strong>proof</strong>, an argument so airtight that
    once seen, it can never be un-seen. Proof is what separates mathematics from every
    other empirical subject. A physics claim is held until the next experiment; a
    mathematical claim, once proven, holds for all time and all universes where its
    definitions make sense. This page is the craft manual: the small logical vocabulary
    every proof is written in, the four great proof patterns, and the discipline of writing
    arguments that leave no exit for doubt. It is also the toolkit behind the geometry unit
    this lesson sits in — every circle theorem you know is a proof you can now dissect.
  </p>
  <p>
    The atoms are <strong>propositions</strong> — statements that are definitely true or
    false (2 is prime: true; 7 is even: false; "this sentence is false": neither, and
    therefore not a proposition). Connectives build compound propositions: AND, OR, NOT,
    and the all-important <strong>implication</strong> "if P then Q", written P → Q. The
    implication deserves care, because its everyday and technical meanings differ. In
    logic, P → Q is false in exactly one case: P true and Q false. If the hypothesis is
    false, the implication is vacuously true — "if pigs fly, I am the Pope" is a true
    statement. That convention is not pedantry; it is what makes proofs by cases and
    contrapositives work cleanly.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> a vacuous implication is false, or meaningless — that
  "if 7 is even, then 7 is prime" must be wrong because 7 is not even. In logic it is
  simply true: the promise "Q whenever P" has never been broken, because the condition
  never arose. The convention feels backwards only if you read P → Q as causation; it
  records truth values, not mechanisms.</p>

  <h2>Implications: converse, inverse, contrapositive</h2>
  <p>
    From any implication P → Q, three cousins can be formed, and keeping them straight is
    half of careful reasoning:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Converse</strong> (Q → P): <em>not</em> equivalent. "If it rains, the pitch is wet" does not mean "if the pitch is wet, it rained" — the sprinklers ran this morning.</li>
    <li><strong>Inverse</strong> (¬P → ¬Q): also not equivalent, and in fact equivalent to the converse.</li>
    <li><strong>Contrapositive</strong> (¬Q → ¬P): <em>always logically equivalent</em> to the original. "If the pitch is dry, it did not rain" says exactly what the original says.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The equivalence of P → Q and ¬Q → ¬P can be checked by truth table: both are false in
    exactly one row (P true, Q false) and true in the other three. This single fact powers
    an entire proof technique, because sometimes the contrapositive is dramatically easier
    to argue. Example: "if n² is even, then n is even." Trying it forwards means squaring
    an arbitrary number and inspecting parity — clumsy. The contrapositive, "if n is odd,
    then n² is odd," is one line: n odd means n = 2m + 1, so n² = 4m² + 4m + 1 = 2(2m² + 2m) + 1,
    which is visibly odd. Done — and the original claim comes free. A related trap has a
    name: <strong>affirming the consequent</strong>. "If the equation has det = 0 it has no
    inverse; this matrix has no inverse; therefore its det is 0" — invalid, because
    non-invertibility might arise another way (it cannot here, but the inference form is
    still illegal; the converse is doing the work). Its twin, <strong>denying the
    antecedent</strong>, fails the same way.
  </p>
  <p>
    Quantifiers complete the vocabulary. "For all x, P(x)" and "there exists x, P(x)"
    behave oppositely under negation: the negation of "all primes are odd" is "there
    exists a prime that is even" — and a single example (2) settles it. The negation of
    "there is a largest prime" is "every prime has a bigger prime" — which is exactly what
    Euclid proved. Flipping quantifiers correctly is the grammar of disproof.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the vocabulary is in place — implications and their three
    cousins, quantifiers and their negations — and the contrapositive equivalence emerged as
    the first genuinely load-bearing identity. Next we turn vocabulary into technique: four
    proof patterns, each a disciplined way of deploying exactly these moves.
  </p>

  <h2>The four proof patterns</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Direct proof.</strong> Chain known facts into the claim. Worked example:
    "the sum of two even integers is even." Let m = 2a and n = 2b (that is what "even"
    <em>means</em> — definitions are load-bearing). Then m + n = 2a + 2b = 2(a + b), which
    is 2 times an integer, hence even. The proof is three lines because the definition was
    deployed precisely; most "stuck" proofs are stuck on an unexamined definition.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Proof by contrapositive.</strong> As above: prove ¬Q → ¬P and claim P → Q by
    the truth-table identity. Best when Q is an awkward property (like "even") and ¬Q
    gives you something concrete (like "odd", with its algebraic form 2m + 1).
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Proof by contradiction (reductio ad absurdum).</strong> Assume the claim is
    false, derive anything impossible, and conclude the claim was true — the only way a
    false assumption can hurt you is by contradiction. The most famous proof in
    mathematics runs this way:
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="the classic reductio — assume √2 is a fraction, watch the assumption destroy itself" widget="equationStep" config={sqrt2Cfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the six cards are one self-destruct sequence — read them
    as a chain of forced moves, not a list of facts. Card 1 makes the assumption in its
    strongest form (lowest terms, no common factor), and every later card uses nothing more
    than elementary parity. Watch card 5 in particular: nothing exotic appears, just a and b
    both even — which contradicts the very form the assumption demanded. The final
    conclusion is not "probably" or "to many decimal places"; it is the only consistent
    escape from an impossible premise.
  </p>

  <p>
    Euclid's infinitude of primes is the other monument, and it is short enough to run in
    your head. Suppose the primes are finite: p₁, p₂, …, pₙ is the complete list. Form
    N = p₁·p₂·…·pₙ + 1. Divide N by each prime in turn: each leaves remainder 1, so none
    divides N. So N is either itself prime (a prime missing from the "complete" list) or
    has a prime factor missing from the list. Either way, contradiction. Hence no complete
    finite list exists — primes are inexhaustible. Notice the structure: no new primes were
    computed, no formula found; a single impossible consequence demolished the assumption.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Mathematical induction.</strong> For claims of the form "P(n) holds for every
    natural number n": verify P(1) (the base case), then prove the <strong>inductive
    step</strong> — that P(k) implies P(k+1). The domino picture explains why this
    suffices: the base case tips domino 1, the step guarantees each falling domino tips
    the next, so every domino falls. The full walk-through for the oldest formula in the
    book:
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="mathematical induction — the domino argument, run on the oldest formula in the book" widget="equationStep" config={indCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the four cards are the domino argument in miniature.
    Card 1 lines the claim up for every n at once; card 2 tips the first domino by direct
    arithmetic; card 3 assumes P(k) for one arbitrary k — a legitimate conditional
    assumption, not the thing being proved; card 4 derives P(k+1) from it, which is the
    entire inductive step. Check that the step never used anything special about k: that
    generality is precisely what lets the argument repeat forever.
  </p>

  <p>
    Induction proves <em>that</em>, never <em>why</em> — it verifies the formula without
    revealing where it came from (the geometric picture of a staircase of dots, or Gauss's
    pairing argument 1 + n, 2 + (n−1), …, gives the insight). Also note what induction
    does <em>not</em> require: you do not "assume the thing you are proving" — you assume
    P(k) for one arbitrary k, which is exactly the conditional structure P(k) → P(k+1).
    Confusing those is the most common objection from sharp beginners, and the truth-table
    view dissolves it.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> the base case is a formality — once the inductive step
  works, the claim holds. Without the base case no domino is ever tipped, and "inductions"
  with a broken first step produce classics: the famous bogus proof that all horses are the
  same colour runs a valid step for every group size except the jump from 1 to 2, where it
  fails. A working step plus a verified base is the whole theorem; either one alone proves
  nothing.</p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> each pattern has been demonstrated on a monument — the reductio
    on √2, Euclid's infinitude argument, induction on the oldest formula in the book. Next
    the same discipline is applied to a syllabus theorem you already know, so you can watch
    a proof you once memorised being assembled line by justified line.
  </p>

  <h2>Proof at work: one circle theorem end to end</h2>
  <p>
    Since this lesson lives in the geometry unit, here is the method applied to a syllabus
    theorem — <strong>the angle in a semicircle is a right angle</strong>. Claim: if AB is
    a diameter of a circle with centre O and C is any other point on the circle, then angle
    ACB = 90°. Proof: join OC. Because OC and OA are both radii, triangle OAC is isosceles,
    so its base angles are equal — call them α. Similarly triangle OBC is isosceles with
    base angles β. Now the angle AOC sits outside triangle OAC, so by the exterior-angle
    identity it equals 2α; likewise angle BOC = 2β. But angles AOC and BOC together make
    the straight line AOB, so 2α + 2β = 180°, hence α + β = 90°. And α + β <em>is</em>
    angle ACB. Three ingredients — isosceles base angles, the exterior-angle identity, and
    angles on a straight line — assembled with every step justified. This is the template:
    the diagram suggested the radii, the definitions did the rest.
  </p>

  <h2>Counterexamples: the one-line demolition</h2>
  <p>
    No number of confirming examples proves a universal claim, but a single
    <strong>counterexample</strong> destroys it. The danger is that patterns can run
    breathtakingly long before breaking. Euler noted that n² − n + 41 yields a prime for
    every n from 1 to 40 — forty consecutive successes — and fails at n = 41, where the
    value is 41² (41 divides it). So testing, however extensive, is not proof; it is
    suspicion with data. Counterexamples are also constructive tools: proving "not all
    numbers are of the form…" <em>is</em> exhibiting one number that is not. Mathematicians
    collect counterexamples the way engineers collect failure reports — each one maps the
    boundary of a theorem, which is why the great counterexample collections (like Gelbaum
    and Olmsted's) are genuine research references.
  </p>

  <h2>Writing clean proofs: the discipline</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>State the claim and the type of argument before starting.</strong> "We prove the contrapositive" tells the reader the road map.</li>
    <li><strong>Define every variable before using it.</strong> "Let n be an integer" — the proof dies without the quantifier, because the claim might only hold for positive n.</li>
    <li><strong>Each line needs a reason.</strong> A previous line, a definition, a named theorem, or algebra. If a step feels too obvious to justify, write the justification anyway — obviousness is where errors live.</li>
    <li><strong>Draw the picture, write the algebra.</strong> In geometry — the unit this lesson belongs to — the diagram suggests, the proof confirms. The theorem "the angle at the centre of a circle is twice the angle at the circumference subtended by the same arc" is suggested by any compass drawing and proven by the isosceles-triangle fact that radii make equal base angles, plus the exterior-angle identity. Never let the diagram substitute for the argument: misleading figures are a documented source of false "proofs".</li>
    <li><strong>Prove backwards, write forwards.</strong> Discover the path by working from the goal, then present it from the givens. A proof is a performance of understanding, not the diary of finding it.</li>
  </ul>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> a carefully drawn, accurate-looking diagram can serve as
  the proof, or at least as strong evidence. Geometry is littered with "proofs by picture"
  that survive every eye test and fail at one unlabelled point — a triangle secretly not
  general, an angle assumed by the drawing software. The diagram proposes; only the
  argument disposes, which is why every claim in a written proof must point to a
  justification, never to the figure.</p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Recall: in a proof by induction, what exactly may you assume in the inductive step, and what exactly must you derive?</li>
    <li>Application: Euler's polynomial n² − n + 41 gives a prime for every n from 1 to 40. Is the claim "this polynomial is prime for every natural number n" proven, and how would you settle it?</li>
    <li>Why does one counterexample destroy a universal claim while no number of confirming examples can establish it?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) You may assume P(k) for one arbitrary k — not the full
    claim — and must derive P(k+1) from that assumption alone, using nothing special about
    k; the base case tips the first domino. (2) Not proven — testing is suspicion with data.
    At n = 41 the polynomial equals 41², which factors: Euler's counterexample, forty
    consecutive successes long, settles the universal claim in one line of disproof. (3) A
    universal claim asserts every case, so a single failing case falsifies it completely;
    confirming cases, however many, never touch the untested ones. That asymmetry is why the
    counterexample is the one-line demolition — and why forty primes proved nothing.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Proof is an argument so airtight it can never be un-seen — the thing separating mathematics from every empirical subject.</li>
    <li>P → Q is false in exactly one case (P true, Q false); vacuous implications are true, and the convention is what makes contrapositive proofs work.</li>
    <li>The contrapositive is logically equivalent to the original; the converse and inverse are not — confusing them is the classic fallacy.</li>
    <li>Four patterns: direct proof (definitions deployed precisely), contrapositive, contradiction, and induction — base case plus step, or nothing falls.</li>
    <li>One counterexample destroys a universal claim; no number of confirming examples establishes one.</li>
    <li>Clean proof hygiene: state the road map, define every variable, justify every line, let the diagram propose but never dispose.</li>
    <li>Gödel: any consistent system rich enough for arithmetic contains true statements it cannot prove — mathematics is inexhaustible.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The advanced lesson <em>Number Theory</em> is the natural next stop: Euclid's
    infinitude argument and induction become its daily tools, and its RSA material shows a
    proof-grade theorem running the internet. The advanced lessons <em>Integration</em> and
    <em>Differential Equations</em> supply claims — the fundamental theorem, existence and
    uniqueness — that this page now lets you see as theorems rather than announcements.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Axioms and the shape of mathematics.</strong> Every proof rests finally on
    unproved starting points — <strong>axioms</strong>. Euclid built geometry on five
    postulates; two millennia later it emerged that the fifth (the parallel postulate)
    could be denied without contradiction, producing the non-Euclidean geometries on which
    general relativity is built. The lesson is profound: proofs are conditional on axioms,
    and changing axioms changes the universe of true statements. Within arithmetic,
    <strong>Peano's axioms</strong> generate the natural numbers, and induction is literally
    one of the axioms — which is why it is so powerful and why it applies only to the
    integers (it fails for claims about the reals, which are not generated by a discrete
    first element plus a successor).
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Gödel's incompleteness theorems.</strong> In 1931 Kurt Gödel proved that any
    consistent axiom system rich enough to describe arithmetic contains true statements it
    cannot prove, and — the second shock — cannot prove its own consistency. The proof is
    a reductio in disguise: encode the sentence "this statement is unprovable" as a
    number-theoretic claim; if the system proves it, the system is inconsistent; if it
    cannot, the statement is true but unprovable. Mathematics is thereby shown to be
    inexhaustible: no finite rulebook captures all truth. Hilbert's dream of a complete,
    self-certifying foundation ended — and in its place came a sharper question, namely
    <em>which</em> statements a given system can settle. That question matured into
    computability theory and the P versus NP problem, since a proof is precisely a
    certificate that can be checked fast even if finding it may be hard.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Constructive versus non-constructive proof.</strong> Classical logic lets you
    prove "there exists x with P(x)" by contradiction — without ever exhibiting x.
    Constructivist mathematicians reject that move and demand a construction; the debate,
    once philosophical, turned practical: computer science distinguishes existence proofs
    that come with an algorithm from those that do not, and complexity theory quantifies
    the gap. Related frontier: <strong>proof assistants</strong> (Lean, Coq, Isabelle) now
    machine-check proofs — the four-colour theorem (1976) was among the first whose proof
    no human could fully read, and Fermat's Last Theorem's 100-plus-page argument has been
    fully formalised. The definition of "airtight" is becoming executable.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Open territory.</strong> Long-standing conjectures — the Riemann hypothesis,
    the twin-prime conjecture, P versus NP — remain in the state every mathematician
    works in: overwhelming evidence, no proof. Yitang Zhang's 2013 bounded-gaps result
    (infinitely many prime pairs closer together than 70 million, since driven down to
    246) shows the texture of progress: the full twin-prime claim still stands, but the
    method it inspired reshaped analytic number theory. Learning proof is joining a
    conversation that has run for twenty-three centuries — and in which, unlike every
    other field, nothing once established is ever revised.
  </p>

